//! stage:typing no-std
// Companion of attack-01, found in review. expected: rejected at every stage (the verdict of FULL).
// The same program with the alias on the goal instead of the given: `summon[BB]` asks for B, the
// given has type A, the pair (A, B) is the pair of the whole types, and `match` refuses it, although
// A == B follows from T == A, T == B. Compare attack-01, where the alias on the given makes the same
// equality usable. Under FULL,eqindex the index skips the given here too, rightly. The two programs
// must keep their two verdicts until the designers decide which is intended (open question 5).
// currently: base rejected (summon.subgoals=6 summon.matches=444); FULL rejected (summon.subgoals=4 summon.matches=294 index.fallback=4); stage 1 as first implemented (binary eq15, FULL,eqindex,eqeager) rejected (summon.subgoals=4 summon.matches=22). FULL says: no coercion from '<T> T ==> [Void](let Metatype<T>) let -> yield T' to '[Void](let Metatype<B>) let -> yield B. With selfcheck: no violation.
struct A { public memberwise init }
struct B { public memberwise init }
type BB = B
fun use<T>(x: T) {}
fun f<T where T == A, T == B>() {
  given let a: A = A()
  use(summon[BB])
}
